 "title": "A Storytelling Technique to Make Your Business Memorable",
 "topic": "content creation",
 "summary": "The creator explains an unconventional storytelling technique where you start client stories with the most important or surprising outcome instead of the beginning. This builds curiosity and helps affluent prospects remember your business distinctively.",
 "key_points": [
  "Don't start client stories with the predictable sequence of problem, evaluation, and solution.",
  "Start your story with the most important accomplishment or a surprising moment.",
  "Example opening sentence: The most important thing we accomplished wasn't what the client originally hired us to do.",
  "People forget credentials and services, but they remember interesting stories that reveal how you work.",
  "Action step: Think of a successful client engagement and identify the most surprising, meaningful, or unexpected moment in one sentence.",
  "Use that single sentence as the opening of your next reel, email, or client story to make people curious."
 ],
 "content": "Here’s a creative and somewhat unusual storytelling technique that can make your business considerably more memorable to affluent prospects. Don't start your client's stories at the beginning. Start with the moment that matters most. Now, most businesses tell clients stories in a predictable sequence. A client came to us with a problem. We evaluated the situation, we recommended a solution, did the work, and the client was delighted with the results. Sound familiar? Now, there’s nothing wrong with that sequence except that it isn’t particularly interesting and doesn't give someone much reason to keep listening. So, here’s a different approach. Imagine beginning a client's story with this sentence: The most important thing we accomplished wasn’t what the client originally hired us to do. Now, you’ve created a reason for someone to want to know what happened and only then do you explain the situation, what the client was trying to accomplish, and how your business helped. Now, here’s why this man for your brand. People may forget your credentials, your list of services, or even the impressive results that you describe, but they are much more likely to remember an interesting story that reveals something distinctive about the way you work and here’s a tip you can use immediately. Think about one of your most successful client engagements and ask yourself, what was the most surprising, meaningful, or unexpected moment? Write that moment down in one sentence. Then, what I’d like to have you do is make it the opening of your next real Email or client story. Don’t begin with who the client was or what they hired you to do. Make people curious about what happened and then tell them how you got there. Now, you’re not simply making your marketing more entertaining You’re creating memorable associations with your business that can influence who affluent prospects think of when they’re ready to hire someone. So, if you’d like to learn how create a distinctive, memorable brand that attracts affluent clients, well, go ahead and comment brand and I’ll send you information about my video program, The Ultimate Branding Blueprint. Comment brand and I’ll send the link over to you. This is Mark Satterfield. Hope you found this helpful. I’ll talk to you soon. Bye for now.",
